#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045486 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/hard #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045499 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/text #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1007 "Linear Algebra" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Linear Algebra course builds on students' prior experience with matrices and determinants to develop their understanding of crucial linear algebra concepts. These include systems of equations, vector spaces, linear transformations, eigenvectors and eigenvalues, diagonalization, matrix functions, inner products, orthogonal projections, quadratic forms, and singular value decomposition. Real-world applications such as least-squares problems, Markov chains, and regression will also be explored.\nThis course is designed to give students a deep understanding of linear algebra, equipping them with the knowledge and skills necessary for advanced study in mathematics, computer science, physics, and engineering." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1009 "After briefly looking at some essential set theory, logic, and vector geometry, students explore matrices in-depth. They will become fluent in Gaussian elimination, using it to solve systems of equations and compute inverse matrices.\nAs part of this course, students perform a deep dive into vector spaces. They will explore vectors in N-dimensional space, linear independence, subspaces, bases, dimension, rank, nullity, and the rank-nullity theorem. They also generalize key concepts to abstract vector spaces and inner product spaces.\nThis course develops students' understanding of linear transformations, including images, kernels, linear maps between different vector spaces, and change-of-coordinates matrices.\nStudents will learn how to find and solve the characteristic equation of a matrix and fluently compute eigenvectors. They will study matrix diagonalization and its properties, apply the rotational-scaling theorem, and explore matrix functions. They will also cover generalized eigenvectors and Jordan canonical decomposition.\nThis course covers various aspects of orthogonality in vector spaces, including orthogonal sets, complements, and orthogonal matrices. In addition, students will learn about orthogonal projections and the Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization process.\nStudents will explore quadratic forms, including positive-definite and negative-definite quadratic forms, and the diagonalization of symmetric matrices. They will also classify quadratic curves and learn how to reduce a quadratic curve to its principal axes.\nFinally, this course explores singular value decomposition and other applications of linear algebra, such as linear least-squares problems, Markov chains, and regression." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1012 55 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1013 "LAL" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046669 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "f4899d31-ceac-5f10-b529-994cde2dd912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048342 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048342 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048342 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048342 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186229141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048342 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Second-Order ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048342 #edb/ref 1033 6704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048342 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "971f4f1b-99c0-555e-8afb-feeef118b307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428275 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1031 "The Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1033 152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1034 0.6386989650323192 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d58eda6d-18a9-51fc-a2ae-3720429fd25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428342 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1031 "Cramer’s Rule for 3x3 Systems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1033 1776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049788 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1420f0fa-c9b8-5cba-8bba-e14cbf53c041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1031 "Cramer's Rule for 2x2 Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1033 1775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050129 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2eee4710-3f26-5186-948b-a819cfaf4083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119599 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119599 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y &=-1 \\\\ -3x+3y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nExpress $y$ in terms of $2 \\times 2$ determinants." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4efedc4e-19e9-552c-bc27-814df953e141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119599 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y &=-1 \\\\ -3x+3y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_y$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119601 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119601 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\langle x,3 \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y &=4 \\\\ x+ky &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. ![](images/7d/7d767f03d04f1c35a774ef089d706894b2fe426995f1c7c050f8c5af0ef9e1e2.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/5a/5a9fe2dde8c8202f4532f745f0fac58ec649bdf39e928560ccaa2485d0f6f09e.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/7e/7e2a9ca703f25107de026c84a8e7feb6099b48c7794ffdc166108358701feb8f.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119601 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "311ba12f-de22-580c-9e0d-6766f7864d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119601 #edb/ref 1074 "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\dfrac{D_y}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{36}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}| & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119658 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119658 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=2 \\\\ 2x+y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as\n![](images/f5/f5c9b1c83ed648b56b47bd370ba5b15359521492087eda769d4122be99384a0f.svg)\nExpress $D_x$ in terms of a $2 \\times 2$ determinant." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119658 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d81e32a1-bd93-5a95-bb4a-e8df9063b5aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119658 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=2 \\\\ 2x+y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing an Unknown in Terms of 2x2 Determinants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119788 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4fcf3704-4856-5095-a6f4-7ec20d81f3c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119790 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Parts of Cramer's Rule Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119844 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e7580024-f357-57d4-94f2-85c25e2c144b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1045 "Cramer's Rule for 2x2 Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119997 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "acb4d472-a6fc-5c2c-a30f-1918865acaa4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477629 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477630 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477631 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120448 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120448 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120448 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $⟨x,3⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=10 \\\\ kx-4y &=12.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\nI. $3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|$\n\nII. $|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|$\n\nIII. $|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120448 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38525400-a144-5244-a7f9-f068d294beba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120448 #edb/ref 1074 "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120647 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120647 #edb/ref 1061 16704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cb786bc-aa30-57b8-aa52-e09a7018f11b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120650 #edb/ref 1061 16710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120650 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0691d00f-77e7-57d5-b71b-b1153b0b4afa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120650 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120652 #edb/ref 1061 16830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48887938-d6a0-5822-b084-3bf10a3a4461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120731 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120731 #edb/ref 1061 19262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120731 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "720a6f93-380c-5e70-8c81-e99f44a4ef11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120731 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121027 #edb/ref 1064 "**Cramer's Rule** is a method for solving a linear system of equations using determinants.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y &={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/c7/c7ef883a1d4dd8c1fc0fc8fbde5ed80c9a5229650fc77c77559b71552370c35b.svg) Then Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\nx =  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{D_y}{D}\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n![](images/b1/b13ecd10395fe0977d04d627179667cbca207fcf4015a89d43df0e69765f1f5e.svg)\n\nFor example, let's use Cramer's rule to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y &=-1 \\\\ -1x+2y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the **determinant of the coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $D_x$ and $D_y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{4}{4}=1, \\\\ y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{8}{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is common to write the solutions to a linear system as a vector. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y \\right> = \\left< 1,2 \\right>.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186121027 #edb/ref 1065 3102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121027 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3adc5c1c-58e5-5c0a-a8f7-da6abd6f06e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121027 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464973 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186271991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464974 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186271991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186464975 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186271991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271991 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $⟨x,-2⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}15x+8y &=-1 \\\\ kx+13y &=-31.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n- **I.** $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=2|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- **II.** $|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n- **III.** $-2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddc658b6-e0a7-590f-af72-c80671fc917f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271991 #edb/ref 1074 "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ -2|\\begin{aligned}15 & 8 \\\\ k & 13\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}15 & -1 \\\\ k & -31\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327020 #edb/ref 1080 "|\\begin{aligned}12 & 10 \\\\ -7 & -14\\end{aligned}|" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327020 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327020 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09faa4b6-c5b4-5f03-9e0b-0e8662ae5749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327634 #edb/ref 1080 "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327634 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327634 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5083ccb3-327d-53a5-8c33-8e6294c999da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327654 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 26 & 5\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327654 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1